Yan Caoa,b, Yunxia Zhaoa, Fujiao Songa, Qin Zhonga∗
a. School of Chemical Engineering, Nanjing University of Science and Technology, Nanjing 210094, Jiangsu, China; b. School of Environmental Science and Engineering, Yancheng Institute of Technology, Yancheng 224000, Jiangsu, China [ Manuscript received November 1, 2013; revised December 9, 2013 ]
Abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted much attention as adsorbents for the separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from flue gas or natural gas. Here, a typical metal-organic framework HKUST-1(also named $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC or MOF-199) was chemically reduced by doping it with alkali metals (Li, Na and K) and they were further used to investigate their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities. The structural information, surface chemistry and thermal behavior of the prepared adsorbent samples were characterized by $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray powder diffraction (XRD), thermo-gravimetric analysis (TGA) and nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherm analysis. The results showed that the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ storage capacity of HKUST-1 doped with moderate quantities of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ and $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , individually, was greater than that of unmodified HKUST-1. The highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption uptake of 8.64 $\mathrm{{mmol/g}}$ was obtained with 1K-HKUST-1, and it was ca. $11\%$ increase in adsorption capacity at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 18 bar as compared with HKUST1. Moreover, adsorption tests showed that HKUST-1 and 1K-HKUST-1 displayed much higher adsorption capacities of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ than those of ${\bf N}_{2}$ . Finally, the adsorption/desorption cycle experiment revealed that the adsorption performance of 1K-HKUST-1 was fairly stable, without obvious deterioration in the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ after 10 cycles.
## Key words
metal-organic framework; HKUST-1; carbon dioxide adsorption; alkali metals cation doping; adsorption-desorption cycles
## 1. Introduction
Since the industrial revolution the fossil fuel-dependent development has resulted in rapid increase of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration in the atmosphere, which has caused the global climate changes [1]. Carbon reduction has become a hot research area of experts from all walks of life over the world [2]. Nowadays, there are various approaches of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation and storage, such as solvent absorption, chemical [3,4] and physical adsorption [5], membranes separation [6], cryogenic distillation [7], and so on. Compared with large-scale separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ by liquid phase amine-based absorption, physisorption between solid adsorbents and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules is a reversible process that requires much less energy for desorption [8]. Thus, development of highly efficient solid sorbents for the capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has become more and more important.
Traditional solid adsorbents include activated carbons [9], zeolites [10], hydrotalcite [11], metal carbides [12,13], and amine-functioned porous materials [14]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) as a novel type of porous crystal materials, have attracted extensive attention around the world, due to their extraordinary adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ [15]. MOFs are a type of novel crystal materials with periodic network structure, formed by self-assembly through covalent bond or inter-molecular forces between metal ions and organic ligands [16]. They have similar structures and properties to zeolites. Up to now, many MOF materials have been synthesized and tested for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. MOF-2 was the first MOF material which was used for studying $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of MOF-2, as reported in Ref. [17], at 1 atm and $195\mathrm{K}$ was $2\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ . Then, it was reported that MOF177 exhibited a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $33.5\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 40 bar [15], which exceeded the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities of zeolites and activated carbons reported in literatures. Among the various MOFs reported, HKUST-1, firstly reported by Chui et al. [18], was assembled by the coordinate bond between $\mathtt{C u}$ ions and tricarboxylate $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ [18,19]. This MOF is easily synthesized by cheap primary compounds and is also commercially available now. HKUST-1 has been attracting much attention in both experimental research and theoretical simulation [20−23]. Millward et al. [21] evaluated the gravimetric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities on HKUST-1 in a range of 0–42 bar at ambient temperature, and they found an approximately fivefold higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption amount on HKUST-1 than that on MOF-177 under low pressure conditions. Xie et al. [22] tested the adsorption capacity of HKUST-1 and claimed that its adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was about $69\mathrm{mL/g}$ at $295\mathrm{K}$ and low pressure. Moreover, they indicated that the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ on HKUST-1 cannot be significantly influenced in the presence of main flue gas components such as $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ , NO and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Yang et al. [23] performed a computational study on the adsorption separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from flue gases with a simulated mixture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ on HKUST-1. The computational results showed that HKUST-1was a promising MOF material for the separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from flue gases, and gas compositions were important factors in optimizing the process of adsorption separation of gas mixtures. Taking the reported high thermal and structural stability of HKUST-1 and its excellent performance on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption into account, HKUST-1 is considered as one of the promising adsorbents for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation from flue gas [24,25].
In order to increase $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity together with the adsorption selectivity to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over other gases, various modifications have been applied to MOFs, such as cation exchange [26], formation of MOF-inorganic species composites (such as carbon nanotube [27], graphite oxide [28], and silica [29]) and the grafting of amine groups onto the coordinatively unsaturated MOF sites. Lan et al. [30] used the multiscale simulation approach and indicated that Li was the best surface modifier of covalent organic frameworks (COFs) for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption among a series of metals such as alkali metals (Li, Na, K), alkaline-earth metals (Be, $\mathbf{Mg}$ , Ca) and transition metals (Sc, Ti). Furthermore, their simulations showed that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes of the lithium-doped COFs could be enhanced by four to eight times compared with the undoped COFs at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar. Xiang et al. [31] prepared the hybrid $\mathrm{Li}@\mathrm{CNT}@[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ , which was formed by the combination of Li doping and carbon nanotube incorporation. Toward this material $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptakes per effective specific surface area increased about $305\%$ and $200\%$ , respectively, compared with the unmodified MOF. Zhao et al. [32] synthesized the composites of HKUST-1 with aminated graphite oxide (MOF/GO-U). The MOF/GO-U3 composite exhibited an excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity which is about $4.23\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar. Choi et al. [33] modified open metal coordination sites of $\mathrm{Mg/DOBDC}$ with ethylene diamine (ED) to introduce pendent amines into MOF micropores. This modified MOF, ED-Mg/DOBDC, had both improved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity at ultra-dilute $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ partial pressures and increased stability/regenerability.
To the best of our knowledge, few investigations have been focused on the influence of metal-doping on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, although this metal-doping strategy has been extensively studied in hydrogen storage which has been proven to be effective [34,35]. These motivate us to look into the effect of metals doping in MOFs on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. In this study, we evaluate the impact of doping of alkali metals (Li, Na and K)
in HKUST-1 on $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture. The adsorption properties of single-component $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ or ${\bf N}_{2}$ were measured by static volumetric method on a homemade high-pressure adsorption apparatus. Moreover, the effect of water vapor on the structural stability was also evaluated. Additionally, the regenerability of the prepared adsorbents was examined via $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cyclical adsorption/desorption experiment.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Synthesis of HKUST-1
The synthesis and activation route of HKUST-1 was mainly followed the procedure reported by Chui et al. [18] with some amendments. Generally, 1,3,5-benzene tricarboxylic acid, $\mathrm{H_{3}B T C(2\mathrm{~g},\ 9.5\ m m o l)}$ was added into $60\mathrm{mL}$ DMF/ethanol $(1:1\ \mathrm{v/v})$ and stirred at room temperature for $10\mathrm{min}$ , then a solution of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(4.154\mathrm{g}$ , $17.1\mathrm{mmol}$ ) in water $\ensuremath{\mathrm{30}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{mL}})$ was added into the mixture and stirred for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The resulting mixture was transferred into a $200\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ at a heating temperature of $373\mathrm{K}$ . After cooling slowly to ambient temperature, the blue powder was collected by repeated centrifugation and thorough washing with ethanol three times. After synthesis, the above crystalline materials were immersed inside chloroform solution for $2\mathrm{d}$ and then the solids were filtered. Finally, these materials were dried by heating at $373\mathrm{K}$ under vacuum overnight.
## 2.2. Synthesis of nM-HKUST-1
Lithium nitrate $(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3})$ ), sodium nitrate $(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3})$ or potassium Nitrate $\left(\mathrm{KNO}_{3}\right)$ ) was individually dissolved in DMF. The solution containing ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ or $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ , respectively, was then added into the solutions containing $\mathrm{{H}_{3}\mathrm{{BTC}}}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ The synthesis and activation of alkali metal cation doped HKUST-1 followed the same route of HKUST1. The dark blue product was designated as nM-HKUST-1, where M represents the alkali metal cation (Li, Na and K) and $n$ is related to the ratio of nitrate to pure HKUST-1.
## 2.3. Samples characterization
X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) patterns were taken on a XD-3 diffractometer (Beijing Purkinje General Instrument Co., Ltd, China) using Cu $K_{\alpha}$ radiation $\langle\lambda=0.15406\ \mathrm{nm}\rangle$ ). The tube voltage was $35\mathrm{kV},$ and the current was $20\mathrm{mA}$ . The XRD diffraction patterns were taken in $2\theta$ range of $5^{\mathrm{{o}}}{\sim}80^{\mathrm{{o}}}$ at a scan speed of $8~\mathrm{^{o}/m i n}$ . Thermo-gravimetric analysis (TGA) was conducted with a SDTQ600 thermal analyzer (PerkinElmer Pyris Diamond, America). Analyses were done under a nitrogen atmosphere $\mathrm{\langle30mL/min}$ flow rate) at a heating rate of $10^{\circ}\mathrm{C/min}$ from room temperature to $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . VSorbet 2008S surface area analyzer (Beijing Jinaipu General Instrument Co., Ltd, China) was used to measure the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms. The pore textural properties including the Brunauer-Emmet-Teller surface area $(S_{\mathrm{BET}})$ , specific Langmuir area $(S_{\mathrm{Langmuir}})$ , pore volume $(V_{\mathrm{pore}})$ and pore size distribution were obtained from the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms. The sample was treated in vacuum at $393\mathrm{K}$ for $^{7\mathrm{h}}$ prior to measurement. And then the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms measurement was carried out at $77\mathrm{K}$ . The crystal morphology and particle size of the materials were observed by a JEOLJSM-6380LV scanning electron microscope (SEM). Before test, the samples were treated by gold coating.
## 2.4. $C O_{2}$ adsorption measurements
To measure high-pressure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at $298\mathrm{K}$ up to about 18 bar, a homemade adsorption apparatus which has been applied by Mansoor Anbia [36], was developed as shown in Figure 1 using a static volumetric system. The reactor section made of stainless steel was tailored by Yatai marine engineering co., LTD (Nantong, Jiangsu, China). The pressure sensor was capacitance type with high precision and absolute pressure. The detailed operation and calculation processes were learned from Ref. [37]. $0.5\:\mathrm{g}$ each sample was loaded in the reactor. First, the existing gas inside the system was swept out with helium. Then, Valves 1 and 2 were opened while Valves 3 and 4 were closed. The vacuum pump was then turned on and the system was vacuumed at $373\mathrm{K}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . After degassing, the system was cooled to ambient temperature. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was introduced and adsorbed by opening Valves 1 and 3 and closing the other valves. The pressure of adsorption reactor decreased due to some dead volume in the reactor (including hollow space and connected tubes) and some adsorption. The dead volumes were calculated via helium tests and subtracted from the total pressure change. After measuring, one could calculate the exact pressure decrease caused by the adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Finally, the values of gas adsorption capacity were calculated by MATLAB software.
## 2.5. Stability under humidity conditions
The stability of these samples under humidity conditions was studied by a self-made device. Water droplet controlled by peristaltic pump flowed into a preheater and converted to water vapor. Samples were exposed to this moisture for a certain time and then the microstructures of these samples were characterized by XRD immediately. The stability of the sample could be estimated based on the XRD patterns changes before and after exposing to moisture.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of HKUST-1 and nM-HKUST-1
## 3.1.1. XRD analysis
XRD patterns of HKUST-1 doped with different quantities of Li, Na and K are shown in Figure 2. It is clear from Figure 2 that the main peaks of the prepared HKUST-1, appeared at $2\theta=9.5^{\mathrm{o}}$ , $11.7^{\mathrm{o}}$ , $13.5^{\mathrm{o}}$ , $19.3^{\mathrm{o}}$ and $26.0^{\mathrm{o}}$ , were in consistant with the experimental and calculated patterns of HKUST-1 reported in Refs. [22,23]. It should be noted that the positions of the XRD peaks of HKUST-1 doped with moderate amounts of Li, Na and K (1Li-HKUST-1, 2Li-HKUST-1, 4Li-HKUST-1 1Na-HKUST-1, 2Na-HKUST-1, 4Na-HKUST1, 1K-HKUST-1 and 2K-HKUST-1) were similar to those of undoped HKUST-1. It can be inferred that both undoped HKUST-1 and doped HKUST-1with moderate quantities of Li, Na and K have the same main cubic structures. Therefore, we assume that HKUST-1 doped with alkali metals would not alter the main cubic structure of the material. However, the characteristic peaks became weaker and shifted right when HKUST-1 was doped with excessive quantities of Li, Na and K (8Li-HKUST-1, 8Na-HKUST-1, 6K-HKUST-1 and 8K-HKUST-1). This shift may be caused by the partial interpenetration of alkali ions into HKUST-1 crystals.
## 3.1.2. SEM analysis
In order to observe its surface morphology, HKUST1 samples were examined with a scanning electron microscope (SEM) after gold deposition and the recorded images are illustrated in Figure 3. All the HKUST-1 samples showed the presence of well-shaped, high-quality octahedral morphology with about 10 to $20\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ width. Such regular structure confirmed the good crystallinity of the materials, which had already been demonstrated by XRD patterns. Meanwhile, the average crystal sizes of both Na-HKUST-1 and K-HKUST-1 were higher than that of Li-HKUST-1.
