## 3.1.4. TGA analysis
In addition to having a large adsorption capacity, the successful application of adsorbent materials in flue gas treatment also requires good stability under operational conditions. Three types of samples with suitable specific surface area and micropore volume were chosen to test the thermal stability using thermo-gravimetric analysis (TGA). As seen from Figure 5, the main turning points of the four samples were similar to each other. The first weight loss step below $373\mathrm{K}$ corresponds to the loss of physically adsorbed water, guest water and solvent molecules. After that point, a plateau could be observed from $373\mathrm{K}$ to $543\mathrm{K}$ , indicating the maintenance of the structure within this temperature range. With further rise in the temperature, a sharp weight loss could be observed from the curve, demonstrating the collapse of the structure and the degradation of all the samples to cuprous oxide or $\mathrm{cu}$ metal, which were in good agreement with previous reported studies [18,22]. The weight losses of 1K-HKUST-1 and 1NaHKUST-1 were ca. $42\mathrm{\wt}\%$ , while that of HKUST-1 was as high as $47\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ . The data demonstrates that 1K-HKUST-1 and 1Na-HKUST-1 have better thermal stability than the parent material HKUST-1. Considering the temperature of common flue gas is ca. $353\mathrm{K}$ , HKUST-1 and M-HKUST-1 have enough thermal stability to be employed in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from flue gas.
## 3.2. Gas adsorption properties of HKUST-1 and M-HKUST-1
${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption curves of HKUST-1 and 1K-HKUST-1 are also shown in Figure 6. ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption capacities of the two samples were not so different from each other, but they were much lower than $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake values. Generally, the capacity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption is higher than that for ${\bf N}_{2}$ on MOFs, because the quadruple moment of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is larger than that of ${\bf N}_{2}$ [38]. Due to the similar ${\bf N}_{2}$ capacities of the two samples, clearly, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity to 1K-HKUST-1 was higher than that of pure HKUST-1.
## 3.3. Inf luence of moisture on structural stability of 1KHKUST-1
Both thermal stability and chemical stability of MOFs are very important for the material. In this work, 1K-HKUST-1 was exposed to the same moisture for a certain time to characterize its structural stability by XRD technique. As seen from Figure 7, after 1K-HKUST-1 samples were exposed to water vapor for 1,3,5 and $10\mathrm{{h}}$ , no significant peak changes could be observed from the XRD patterns. Besides, the sharpness of the peaks at $2\theta=9.5^{\mathrm{o}}$ was slightly reduced as time went on. This indicated that the framework of 1K-HKUST-1 is wellmaintained in the presence of moist air. Notably, the crystals changed their color from dark blue to light blue after exposure to moisture, demonstrating that water had recoordinated to the coordinatively unsaturated metal centers in the activated material. After activation through heat or vacuum, its color could reversibly turn back to dark blue, indicating that water could be adsorbed and desorbed on 1K-HKUST-1 expediently.
## 3.4. Adsorbent regenerability
The regenerability of adsorbents is another important parameter for flue gas capture applications, and the adsorbents should retain stable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption performance for prolonged cyclic operation. Recycle test of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption on 1K-HKUST-1 were performed in this work, due to its highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity among the prepared materials. Figure 8 depicts the static adsorption capacity of 1K-HKUST-1 during repetitive cycles of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption at $298\mathrm{K}$ and desorption by vacuum. The readsorption property is a little higher than that of the first adsorption. This may be attributed to that some part of pores reactivated after the first adsorption/desorption experiment, therefore, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas molecules will be more easily transported. It indicates that the adsorption mechanism of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is physical adsorption. The data of cycle test reveal that the performance of 1K-HKUST-1 is fairly stable, without obvious deterioration in the adsorption capacity after 10 cycles of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption.
## 4. Conclusions
A high-pressure static adsorption apparatus is manufactured in this work to measure the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity on the metal organic framework materials HKUST-1 doped with different alkali metals (Li, Na and K). The samples are characterized by ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption, SEM, TGA and XRD methods. It is found that the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ storage capacities of HKUST-1 doped with moderate quantities of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ and $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ are greater than that of unmodified HKUST-1. The highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption is obtained with 1K-HKUST-1 $(8.64\mathrm{mmol/g})$ , representing a $\mathrm{ca.11\%}$ increase in adsorption capacity at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 18 bar. The high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity is owing to the high surface area and pore volume. Moreover, the cycle experiment of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption reveals that the adsorption performance of 1KHKUST-1 is fairly stable without obvious deterioration in the adsorption capacity after 10 cycles.
